Z||000000||Jake_Dragon||10-03-2001||08:38 PM||||Need some help here.<BR>I am looking at purchasing an 85 GT that’s the good news.<BR>The bad news is it has an exhaust leak and is popping real bad.<BR>The previous owner said it is the manifold and has replacements to go with the car.<BR>The popping noise is coming from the between the left side of the motor by the firewall.<BR>I cant see any flame or feel the leak.<BR>My question is would it be dangerous to drive this car at highway speeds for the 50 miles to my house?<BR><P>------------------<BR>1985 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gt.GIF"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||10-03-2001||09:22 PM||||As long as the leak isn't causing exhaust to spray on anything vital, you <I>should</I> be okay. I've been driving my Formula with a cracked manifold since May, with no apparent ill effects. I do know where the crack is, though. Made sure it wasn't going to melt any wires or torch any sound insulation. It sounds like a big Briggs &  Stratton. Need to fix it one of these days. <P>Having said all that... Ed Parks related a story of a silver Formula T-top that burned to the ground because a cracked manifold started a fire.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Good luck. Be careful. YMMV<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||10-03-2001||11:46 PM||||I think it depends on the size of the crack in the manifold.  I would think you should be ok for 50 miles, but it sure couldnt hurt to go throw down the $20 for a auto fire extinguisher just in case you have a problem.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jake_Dragon||10-04-2001||07:21 PM||||Can get it put on a flat bed for $140 think that is the way to go. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"> ||||reg||1
